Input: {"question": "Which person or organization did David Novak work for?", "documents": [{"doc_id": "d1", "text": "\"The Melodic Path: Unraveling the Academic Journey of David Novak\"\n\nIn the realm of American musicology, few names resonate as profoundly as David Novak. A scholar of immense repute, Novak's contributions to the field have been nothing short of remarkable. As we delve into the intricacies of his academic career, one institution stands out as a pivotal chapter in his journey: the University of California, Santa Barbara. Nestled in the picturesque coastal town of Goleta, California, the University of California, Santa Barbara (UCSB) has long been a hub of intellectual curiosity and innovation. It is here, amidst the sun-kissed hills and scenic vistas, that David Novak's academic trajectory intersected with the university's rich cultural landscape. Our narrative begins in the early 2000s, when Novak, already an established musicologist, joined the faculty at UCSB's Department of Music. This appointment marked a significant milestone in his career, as he brought his unique expertise in music theory and cultural analysis to the university's vibrant academic community. One of the most compelling pieces of evidence supporting Novak's affiliation with UCSB lies in his publication record. A cursory glance at his bibliography reveals a plethora of scholarly articles, book chapters, and reviews published during his tenure at the university. For instance, his 2005 article \"The Politics of Musical Identity in Post-War Japan\" (published in the journal _Ethnomusicology_) showcases his meticulous research and nuanced understanding of the complex interplay between music, culture, and politics. Furthermore, Novak's involvement in various academic initiatives and programs at UCSB provides additional evidence of his association with the university. In 2007, he co-organized the \"Music and Identity\" conference, a groundbreaking event that brought together scholars from across the globe to explore the intricate relationships between music, culture, and identity. This conference, held on the UCSB campus, not only underscored Novak's commitment to interdisciplinary research but also highlighted his ability to foster intellectual dialogue and collaboration. Another crucial piece of evidence lies in the university's own records and publications. A review of UCSB's faculty directories, course catalogs, and departmental newsletters from the 2000s confirms Novak's status as a member of the music faculty. Moreover, his name appears in various university press releases and news articles, further solidifying his connection to the institution."}, {"doc_id": "d2", "text": "As David Novak wandered through the winding streets of Modena, the sweet scent of freshly baked tortellini wafted from the local trattorias, mingling with the sound of laughter and lively chatter. It was a far cry from the dusty archives and cramped libraries he was accustomed to, where the only sounds were the whispers of ancient manuscripts and the creaking of worn leather bindings. Yet, it was here, in this charming Italian city, that David had found a new sense of purpose. As a musicologist, David had spent years studying the intricacies of Renaissance music, pouring over scores and treatises in search of hidden patterns and forgotten melodies. But it was his fascination with the therapeutic properties of music that had brought him to Modena, and to the doorstep of the Azienda Unita' Sanitaria Locale Di Modena. David's eyes sparkled as he recalled his first meeting with Dr. Bianchi, the director of the AUSSL's innovative music therapy program. The doctor's warm smile and infectious enthusiasm had won David over, and soon he found himself immersed in a world of sound and healing. Together, they had designed a series of music workshops for patients with Alzheimer's, using the haunting melodies of Monteverdi and the lilting rhythms of Verdi to unlock memories and spar